The historical context surrounding the founding of MCC Theater is that it was established in 1986 by a collective of young actors, writers, and directors who were eager to take a leadership role in their own artistic development. This collective, which included Robert LuPone and Bernard Telsey, among others, initially held peer-based "classes" that led to showcases and eventually to full-scale productions. This marked a significant development in the Off-Broadway theater scene in New York City.

The founding of MCC Theater, along with other Off-Broadway theaters like Playwrights Horizons and Ars Nova, contributed to the growth and diversification of Off-Broadway theater in New York City. These theaters provided a platform for emerging artists to showcase their work, nurture their talents, and push the boundaries of live entertainment. By supporting innovative and risk-taking work, these theaters helped to create a vibrant and dynamic Off-Broadway scene that continues to thrive today.

In particular, MCC Theater's founding in 1986, along with the establishment of Playwrights Horizons and Ars Nova, marked a significant shift in the Off-Broadway landscape. These theaters not only provided a space for new voices and perspectives to be heard but also helped to redefine the boundaries of what was possible in Off-Broadway theater. By fostering a culture of innovation and experimentation, these theaters have played a crucial role in shaping the development of Off-Broadway theater in New York City.
