What is the historical context of the British lease on Hong Kong, including the terms and duration of the lease?
The historical context of the British lease on Hong Kong is as follows:

The British lease on Hong Kong was established in 1898, when China leased the New Territories to the United Kingdom for 99 years. The lease was signed on June 9, 1898, and it came into effect on July 1, 1898. The terms of the lease allowed the British to govern the New Territories, which included the Kowloon Peninsula and the surrounding islands, for a period of 99 years.

The lease was part of the Second Convention of Peking, which was signed between China and the United Kingdom on June 9, 1898. The convention also included the lease of the Kowloon Peninsula, which was leased to the British in 1860.

The duration of the lease was 99 years, which was to expire on July 1, 1997. However, in 1984, the Sino-British Joint Declaration was signed, which paved the way for the transfer of sovereignty over Hong Kong from the United Kingdom to China on July 1, 1997. The declaration stated that Hong Kong would be returned to China and become a Special Administrative Region, with a high degree of autonomy for 50 years.

The terms of the lease were governed by the Second Convention of Peking and the Sino-British Joint Declaration. The lease was subject to certain conditions, including the requirement that the British would not establish any military bases or fortifications in the New Territories, and that they would not interfere with the internal affairs of China.
