What was the unit of heat that was replaced by the joule, and what was its definition and working mechanism?
The unit of heat that was replaced by the joule is the calorie. 

The calorie was defined as the amount of heat required to raise the temperature of one gram of water by one degree Celsius. Its working mechanism was based on the principle that the amount of heat required to raise the temperature of a substance is directly proportional to the mass of the substance and the change in its temperature.
