What is the chemical composition of CFCs and how do their molecular structures contribute to ozone destruction?
Based on the provided context, CFCs (Chlorofluorocarbons) are fully halogenated paraffin hydrocarbons that contain only carbon (C), chlorine (Cl), and fluorine (F). Their molecular structure contributes to ozone destruction because the chlorine atoms in CFCs can be released in the stratosphere, where they can catalytically destroy ozone molecules (O3).
